      <h1>Temperature &amp; Thermal Testing</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Temperature testing has two jobs. <strong>Performance testing</strong> measures
        how the battery behaves across its operating range — the cold tests that produce CCA, and the hot tests that
        expose accelerated aging. <strong>Thermal-abuse testing</strong> pushes the battery past its limits with external
        heat to verify it fails safely rather than catastrophically.
      </div>

      <h2>Why temperature dominates every result</h2>
      <p>
        Temperature is the single biggest external influence on a battery — it raises internal resistance when cold
        and accelerates every degradation reaction when hot. Any performance test must therefore state its
        temperature, and the cold tests (like CCA at −18°C) are deliberately run at the worst case so the number
        reflects real-world capability.
      </p>

      <h2>The two test families</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>What it does</th><th>Typical standard</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Cold performance</strong></td><td>Discharge at low temperature (e.g. CCA at −18°C)</td><td>SAE J537, EN 50342</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>High-temperature aging</strong></td><td>Cycle or hold at elevated temperature to accelerate aging</td><td>IEC 62660</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Thermal abuse</strong></td><td>Expose to external heat to verify no runaway propagation</td><td>UN 38.3, IEC 62133</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Cold vs hot: two different failures</h2>
      <p>
        Cold reduces <em>performance</em> — the battery cannot deliver as much current, but it is not damaged. Heat
        reduces <em>life</em> — every 10°C rise roughly doubles the rate of the aging reactions (a rule of thumb, not a
        fixed law), so a hot battery ages faster permanently. That is why cold is a test of capability, while heat is
        a test of longevity and safety.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> Temperature is the variable that turns a single battery into many different
        batteries — cold, hot, fresh, aged — which is why a test result without its temperature is not a measurement,
        it is a guess.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Cold changes the number, heat changes the life. A buyer or technician who treats
        temperature as part of the spec — not a footnote — reads battery data correctly.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

